        "sourceExtract": "",
        "modelAnswer": "One major factor delaying civil rights progress was the <strong>political defiance legitimised by the Southern Manifesto in 1956</strong>. Signed by 101 Southern congressmen, the manifesto denounced the <em>Brown</em> ruling as an abuse of power infringing states' rights. By giving federal political sanction to resistance, Southern congressmen encouraged state legislatures to enact hundreds of segregationist obstruction statutes. Therefore, this caused progress to be delayed because prominent national politicians legitimised open defiance of the Supreme Court, assuring Southern officials that federal orders could be safely disregarded.<br><br>Furthermore, progress was obstructed by the <strong>violent intimidation and domestic terrorism of the Ku Klux Klan</strong>. With police collusion, the KKK carried out bombings and murders, notably the 1955 murder of 14-year-old Emmett Till in Mississippi. All-white juries routinely acquitted white assailants despite overwhelming evidence. Therefore, this led directly to delayed progress because lethal violence and institutionalised judicial corruption terrorised rural Black communities into abandoning voter registration and integration efforts.<br><br>In addition, progress was impeded by <strong>state-sponsored 'Massive Resistance' and public school closures</strong>. Southern governors exploited the ambiguous phrase 'with all deliberate speed' from <em>Brown II</em> to block integration entirely. In Virginia, Senator Harry Byrd led policies that defunded integrated schools; Prince Edward County closed public schools for five years rather than integrate, funding private academies for whites. Therefore, this forced civil rights progress to stall because state governments proved willing to dismantle public education rather than comply with federal desegregation mandates.",
