        "targetMins": 5,
        "questionText": "Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views about comparing the 1964 Civil Rights Act and 1965 Voting Rights Act in dismantling Jim Crow. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.",
        "stimulus": [],
        "sourceExtract": "<div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 1:</strong> From a modern historical study.</div><div>The 1964 Civil Rights Act represents one of the most comprehensive pieces of legislation in the nation's history. It outlawed discrimination in employment, banned segregation in public accommodations, and empowered the federal government to withhold funding from segregated state programmes. The bill was proposed following major demonstrations in Birmingham, and public support intensified after the March on Washington.</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 2:</strong> From a different historical perspective.</div><div>The Voting Rights Act of 1965, passed shortly after the Selma campaign, was a transformative milestone for the movement. By outlawing literacy tests and authorising federal oversight of voter registration in the South, the Act dismantled the political structures of Jim Crow. This legislation finally gave Black Southerners access to the ballot box, enabling genuine democracy in the region.</div></div>",
        "modelAnswer": "The main difference is that <strong>Interpretation 1</strong> highlights the 1964 Civil Rights Act as \"one of the most comprehensive pieces of legislation\" in US history for outlawing employment discrimination and public segregation. In contrast, <strong>Interpretation 2</strong> emphasises the 1965 Voting Rights Act as the movement’s \"transformative milestone\" because banning literacy tests and introducing federal oversight dismantled Jim Crow’s political foundations.",
