        "type": "4-Mark Why Interpretations Differ",
        "marks": 4,
        "targetMins": 5,
        "questionText": "Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the methods and community impact of the Black Panther Party. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer.",
        "stimulus": [],
        "sourceExtract": "<div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 1:</strong> From a modern historical study.</div><div>The Black Panther Party achieved prominence through its emphasis on armed self-defence and community monitoring. Composed primarily of urban youth, the party directly challenged police practices by conducting armed patrols to observe arrests. This confrontational stance led to several violent encounters with law enforcement, including a fatal shootout in 1967.</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 2:</strong> From a different historical perspective.</div><div>Beyond their militant public image, the Black Panthers dedicated significant resources to community welfare. They operated free health clinics, distributed clothing, and organised food programmes to support low-income neighbourhoods. These grassroots initiatives addressed immediate social needs while promoting Black pride and education among community youth.</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Source B:</strong> From an interview with Black Panther Party co-founder Bobby Seale, recorded in 1988, describing the group's early activities in Oakland in 1966.</div><div>We encountered police officers making an arrest during one of our vehicle patrols. We stopped to monitor the situation, carrying our weapons openly. A small crowd gathered and observed us. We announced that we were the Black Panther Party and were monitoring the police to prevent brutality. When an officer questioned our firearms, we replied that we carried them for self-defence and observation.</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Source C:</strong> From an editorial in The Black Panther, the party's official publication, 1969.</div><div>We established the Free Breakfast for Children program because we recognize that proper nutrition is essential for learning. Our communities have suffered from neglect, and we must address this. The program has improved student performance according to local teachers. We have launched this initiative in several cities and aim to expand it nationwide as a demonstration of community care.</div></div>",
        "modelAnswer": "The interpretations differ because the historians have given weight to different aspects of Black Panther evidence. <strong>Interpretation 1</strong> is supported by <strong>Source B</strong>, in which co-founder Bobby Seale describes armed Oakland patrols \"carrying our weapons openly\" to monitor police arrests. Conversely, <strong>Interpretation 2</strong> is supported by <strong>Source C</strong>, a 1969 <em>Black Panther</em> editorial highlighting how their \"Free Breakfast for Children program\" tackled neighbourhood neglect and \"improved student performance\".",
        "pdfUrl": "/pdfs/mocks/usa/usa_mock_4_question_booklet.pdf",
