        "marks": 4,
        "targetMins": 5,
        "questionText": "Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views about US military failure and soldier morale during the Vietnam War. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.",
        "stimulus": [],
        "sourceExtract": "<div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 1:</strong> From a modern historical study.</div><div>US planners believed that sustained bombing would force Hanoi to abandon the conflict. However, the air campaign failed to halt the flow of troops and supplies to the South. Vietnamese repair crews quickly reconstructed damaged infrastructure or utilised alternative routes. Consequently, the reliance on air power was insufficient to break the communist insurgency's logistics.</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 2:</strong> From a different historical perspective.</div><div>While early US forces consisted largely of motivated volunteers, the growing reliance on conscription altered the army's composition. Many draftees resented their service and questioned the war's purpose. This decline in discipline manifested in widespread drug abuse, insubordination, and instances of violence against officers. High desertion rates reflected a broader loss of belief in the military mission.</div></div>",
        "modelAnswer": "The main difference is that <strong>Interpretation 1</strong> attributes US military failure to strategic air-power limits, arguing the \"air campaign failed to halt the flow of troops and supplies\" because North Vietnamese crews rapidly repaired routes. In contrast, <strong>Interpretation 2</strong> focuses on internal army breakdown, arguing heavy reliance on unwilling conscripts caused a \"decline in discipline\" marked by drug abuse, desertion, and violence against officers.",
