        "questionText": "Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views about religious opposition to the Nazi regime in the years 1933–39. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.",
        "stimulus": [],
        "sourceExtract": "<div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 1:</strong> From a historical study of the Christian Churches in Nazi Germany, published in 1997.</div><div>\"The Christian Churches in Germany were largely successful in resisting the Nazi regime's attempts to achieve total control over religious life. Despite intense pressure, both the Catholic and Protestant Churches prevented the total Nazification of their institutions. The Concordat of 1933 protected the Catholic Church's independence, and when Hitler broke this agreement, the Pope’s encyclical Mit Brennender Sorge was successfully read in churches across the nation, showing the limits of Nazi power. In the Protestant Church, the Confessing Church led a brave struggle that prevented Ludwig Müller's Reich Church from dominating German Protestants, proving that religious faith remained a powerful barrier to totalitarian control.\"</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 2:</strong> From a history of coercion and consent in the Third Reich, published in 2004.</div><div>\"Christian opposition to the Nazi regime was extremely limited, defensive, and ultimately failed to challenge the wider atrocities of the state. The vast majority of church leaders conformed to the regime because they shared Hitler’s hatred of communism and feared the destruction of their institutions. When pastors like Niemöller spoke out, their protests were strictly limited to defending church independence and religious education; they did not protest against the police state, the banning of political parties, or the persecution of the Jews. The state successfully neutralised the churches through selective terror, arresting outspoken individuals while leaving the broader, compliant institutions intact.\"</div></div>",
        "modelAnswer": "The main difference is that <strong>Interpretation 1</strong> argues the Christian Churches were \"largely successful in resisting the Nazi regime's attempts to achieve total control\", citing how the Confessing Church and <em>Mit Brennender Sorge</em> protected religious independence. In contrast, <strong>Interpretation 2</strong> argues that Christian opposition was \"extremely limited, defensive, and ultimately failed to challenge the wider atrocities of the state\", stressing that most leaders conformed and protested only over narrow church matters rather than the police state or persecution of Jews.",
