        "targetMins": 12,
        "questionText": "How useful are Sources B and C for an enquiry into the persecution of disabled people in Nazi Germany in the years 1933–39? Explain your answer, using Sources B and C and your knowledge of the historical context.",
        "stimulus": [],
        "sourceExtract": "<div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Source B:</strong> From a secret report written by a local public health official in Saxony, December 1935.</div><div>\"The implementation of the Law for the Prevention of Hereditarily Diseased Offspring is proceeding smoothly, though we must confront lingering traditional prejudices. In this district, we have processed over eighty applications for compulsory sterilisation this year alone. While some families initially protest out of ignorance, the majority are brought to understand that eliminating hereditary defects is a biological duty to the state. The local courts have been highly cooperative, accepting medical recommendations without delay. However, we require more secure facilities to prevent patients from fleeing before the surgical procedures are completed.\"</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Source C:</strong> An official Nazi propaganda poster published by the Office of Racial Policy in 1938.</div><img src=\"/images/t4_poster.jpg\" alt=\"Office of Racial Policy poster showing the financial cost of hereditary defects, 1938\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; display: block; margin: 8px 0;\"><div>Translated German text: \"60,000 Reichsmarks is what this person suffering from a hereditary defect costs the People's community during his lifetime. Fellow citizen, that is your money too.\"</div></div>",
        "modelAnswer": "Source B is highly useful for an enquiry into how Nazi sterilisation policies were implemented on a local, administrative level. The content reveals that the <em>Law for the Prevention of Hereditarily Diseased Offspring</em> was enforced systematically, with over eighty compulsory sterilisations in one district alone. It is useful because it highlights the complicity of the legal system, noting that local courts cooperated fully by \"accepting medical recommendations without delay\". The provenance of the source—being a secret report by a local public health official in December 1935—increases its usefulness, as it represents a candid, internal communication not intended for public consumption. This allows the historian to see the true obstacles faced by officials, such as \"traditional prejudices\" and the fact that \"families initially protest,\" as well as the dark reality that the state had to use \"secure facilities\" to prevent patients from escaping. Contextually, we know that the Nazis introduced the Sterilisation Law in July 1933, and by 1939, over 400,000 Germans had been forcibly sterilised, validating the scale of persecution described in the source.<br><br>Source C is extremely useful for investigating the propaganda methods used by the Nazi regime to justify the persecution of disabled people. The content shows how the Nazis utilised economic scapegoating, explicitly stating that a disabled person \"costs the national community 60,000 Reichsmarks\". By contrasting a helpless disabled man with an exhausted, healthy worker, the visual imagery was designed to breed resentment among the working class by claiming \"that is your money too\". The provenance of this source—an official propaganda poster published by the Office of Racial Policy in 1938—is highly useful because it reveals how the state actively sought to \"Nazify\" public attitudes towards euthanasia and racial hygiene. It shows a deliberate attempt to prepare the public mind for harsher measures by framing disabled lives purely in terms of financial cost. Contextually, we know that by 1938, Germany was preparing for war, and the state was ruthlessly prioritizing economic efficiency and rearmament, which culminated in the compulsory sterilisation and concentration camp confinement in 1939, making this economic propaganda highly representative of the period's priorities.",
