        "questionText": "Study Interpretations 1 and 2. They give different views about the persecution of disabled people in Nazi Germany in the years 1933–39. What is the main difference between these views? Explain your answer, using details from both interpretations.",
        "stimulus": [],
        "sourceExtract": "<div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 1:</strong> From a history of racial policy in Nazi Germany, 1933–39, published in 1998.</div><div>\"The persecution of minorities and disabled people under the Nazi regime was fundamentally driven by a deeply ingrained biological and racial ideology. From the moment they seized power, the Nazis utilised the legal system and public health networks to implement eugenics policies. The German medical profession was highly complicit, willingly submitting thousands of patients for compulsory sterilisation. The general public largely conformed to these measures because they had been thoroughly indoctrinated by racial hygiene propaganda in schools and media. This pseudoscience convinced ordinary citizens that sterilising the disabled was essential to protect the purity of the Aryan gene pool.\"</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 2:</strong> From a historical study of Nazi social policy, 1933–39, published in 2004.</div><div>\"While racial rhetoric was always present, the driving force behind the escalating persecution of disabled people was economic utility. During the Great Depression, the Nazi state sought to justify its welfare cuts by portraying the vulnerable as a parasitic financial burden on the hard-working taxpayer. Propaganda deliberately targeted the cost of care to build resentment among workers who were themselves enduring long hours and low wages. Active resistance to these policies was minimal because the police state successfully silenced dissent. However, when the regime expanded compulsory sterilisation under the 1933 Law and confined thousands of vulnerable people and 'asocials' inside concentration camps by 1938, families quietly resented these measures, showing that conformity relied on police-state intimidation and secrecy.\"</div></div>",
        "modelAnswer": "The main difference is that <strong>Interpretation 1</strong> argues the persecution of disabled people was \"fundamentally driven by a deeply ingrained biological and racial ideology\" supported by medical complicity and public belief in protecting \"the purity of the Aryan gene pool\". In contrast, <strong>Interpretation 2</strong> argues the primary driver was \"economic utility\", portraying disabled citizens as a \"parasitic financial burden on the hard-working taxpayer\" while relying on police-state fear and secrecy to maintain conformity.",
