        "targetMins": 5,
        "questionText": "Suggest one reason why Interpretations 1 and 2 give different views about the persecution of disabled people in Nazi Germany in the years 1933–39. You may use Sources B and C to help explain your answer.",
        "stimulus": [],
        "sourceExtract": "<div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 1:</strong> From a history of racial policy in Nazi Germany, 1933–39, published in 1998.</div><div>\"The persecution of minorities and disabled people under the Nazi regime was fundamentally driven by a deeply ingrained biological and racial ideology. From the moment they seized power, the Nazis utilised the legal system and public health networks to implement eugenics policies. The German medical profession was highly complicit, willingly submitting thousands of patients for compulsory sterilisation. The general public largely conformed to these measures because they had been thoroughly indoctrinated by racial hygiene propaganda in schools and media. This pseudoscience convinced ordinary citizens that sterilising the disabled was essential to protect the purity of the Aryan gene pool.\"</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 2:</strong> From a historical study of Nazi social policy, 1933–39, published in 2004.</div><div>\"While racial rhetoric was always present, the driving force behind the escalating persecution of disabled people was economic utility. During the Great Depression, the Nazi state sought to justify its welfare cuts by portraying the vulnerable as a parasitic financial burden on the hard-working taxpayer. Propaganda deliberately targeted the cost of care to build resentment among workers who were themselves enduring long hours and low wages. Active resistance to these policies was minimal because the police state successfully silenced dissent. However, when the regime expanded compulsory sterilisation under the 1933 Law and confined thousands of vulnerable people and 'asocials' inside concentration camps by 1938, families quietly resented these measures, showing that conformity relied on police-state intimidation and secrecy.\"</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Source B:</strong> From a secret report written by a local public health official in Saxony, December 1935.</div><div>\"The implementation of the Law for the Prevention of Hereditarily Diseased Offspring is proceeding smoothly, though we must confront lingering traditional prejudices. In this district, we have processed over eighty applications for compulsory sterilisation this year alone. While some families initially protest out of ignorance, the majority are brought to understand that eliminating hereditary defects is a biological duty to the state. The local courts have been highly cooperative, accepting medical recommendations without delay. However, we require more secure facilities to prevent patients from fleeing before the surgical procedures are completed.\"</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Source C:</strong> An official Nazi propaganda poster published by the Office of Racial Policy in 1938.</div><img src=\"/images/t4_poster.jpg\" alt=\"Office of Racial Policy poster showing the financial cost of hereditary defects, 1938\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; display: block; margin: 8px 0;\"><div>Translated German text: \"60,000 Reichsmarks is what this person suffering from a hereditary defect costs the People's community during his lifetime. Fellow citizen, that is your money too.\"</div></div>",
        "modelAnswer": "The interpretations differ because the historians have placed weight on different contemporary sources. <strong>Interpretation 1</strong> gives weight to medical-administrative evidence such as <strong>Source B</strong>, which frames compulsory sterilisation as a \"biological duty to the state\" to eliminate \"hereditary defects\" with full court and medical cooperation. Conversely, <strong>Interpretation 2</strong> gives weight to financial propaganda like <strong>Source C</strong>, which tells workers that a disabled person costs \"60,000 Reichsmarks\" and warns \"Fellow citizen, that is your money too\", supporting the view of economic scapegoating.",
