        "questionText": "How far do you agree with Interpretation 2 about the persecution of disabled people in Nazi Germany in the years 1933–39? Explain your answer, using both interpretations and your knowledge of the historical context.",
        "stimulus": [],
        "sourceExtract": "<div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 1:</strong> From a history of racial policy in Nazi Germany, 1933–39, published in 1998.</div><div>\"The persecution of minorities and disabled people under the Nazi regime was fundamentally driven by a deeply ingrained biological and racial ideology. From the moment they seized power, the Nazis utilised the legal system and public health networks to implement eugenics policies. The German medical profession was highly complicit, willingly submitting thousands of patients for compulsory sterilisation. The general public largely conformed to these measures because they had been thoroughly indoctrinated by racial hygiene propaganda in schools and media. This pseudoscience convinced ordinary citizens that sterilising the disabled was essential to protect the purity of the Aryan gene pool.\"</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Interpretation 2:</strong> From a historical study of Nazi social policy, 1933–39, published in 2004.</div><div>\"While racial rhetoric was always present, the driving force behind the escalating persecution of disabled people was economic utility. During the Great Depression, the Nazi state sought to justify its welfare cuts by portraying the vulnerable as a parasitic financial burden on the hard-working taxpayer. Propaganda deliberately targeted the cost of care to build resentment among workers who were themselves enduring long hours and low wages. Active resistance to these policies was minimal because the police state successfully silenced dissent. However, when the regime expanded compulsory sterilisation under the 1933 Law and confined thousands of vulnerable people and 'asocials' inside concentration camps by 1938, families quietly resented these measures, showing that conformity relied on police-state intimidation and secrecy.\"</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Source B:</strong> From a secret report written by a local public health official in Saxony, December 1935.</div><div>\"The implementation of the Law for the Prevention of Hereditarily Diseased Offspring is proceeding smoothly, though we must confront lingering traditional prejudices. In this district, we have processed over eighty applications for compulsory sterilisation this year alone. While some families initially protest out of ignorance, the majority are brought to understand that eliminating hereditary defects is a biological duty to the state. The local courts have been highly cooperative, accepting medical recommendations without delay. However, we require more secure facilities to prevent patients from fleeing before the surgical procedures are completed.\"</div></div><hr style=\"border:none; border-top:1px dashed #cbd5e1; margin:12px 0;\"><div><div style=\"font-style: italic; margin-bottom: 6px; font-weight: 600;\"><strong>Source C:</strong> An official Nazi propaganda poster published by the Office of Racial Policy in 1938.</div><img src=\"/images/t4_poster.jpg\" alt=\"Office of Racial Policy poster showing the financial cost of hereditary defects, 1938\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; display: block; margin: 8px 0;\"><div>Translated German text: \"60,000 Reichsmarks is what this person suffering from a hereditary defect costs the People's community during his lifetime. Fellow citizen, that is your money too.\"</div></div>",
        "modelAnswer": "Interpretation 2 presents a compelling argument that the escalation of Nazi persecution of disabled people was heavily rooted in economic utility and financial scapegoating. To a significant extent, this view is convincing. During the Great Depression and the subsequent drive for economic recovery, the Nazi state sought to eliminate any expenditure on those they classified as \"unproductive\". This is clearly supported by Source C, which framed disabled individuals as a direct tax drain of \"60,000 Reichsmarks\" on the hard-working \"national community\". From my own knowledge, this economic rationale was built into the Nazi concept of the <em>Volksgemeinschaft</em> (people's community). To qualify for membership, an individual had to be both racially pure and economically productive. The regime actively cut state welfare budgets, and propaganda targeted the costs of keeping asylum patients alive to build resentment among workers who were enduring longer hours and frozen wages under Robert Ley’s German Labour Front (DAF). Thus, the economic framing was a highly practical tool to justify the state's abandonment of the vulnerable.<br><br>However, Interpretation 2 downplays the fundamental role of biological and racial ideology, which is the primary focus of Interpretation 1. As Interpretation 1 correctly asserts, eugenics and \"racial hygiene\" were not merely rhetorical covers for economic cuts; they were the absolute core of Nazi ideology. From my own knowledge, the <em>Law for the Prevention of Hereditarily Diseased Offspring</em>, passed in July 1933, was drafted directly by Nazi racial theorists who believed in the pseudoscience of controlled breeding. The complicity of the German medical profession, highlighted in Source B, proves that doctors and judges cooperated not out of economic pressure, but because they had been captured by the \"biological duty\" to purify the Aryan race. This ideological indoctrination was built directly into the school curriculum, where children were taught \"Race Studies\" and forced to calculate the hereditary costs of disabled people in mathematics textbooks. Therefore, the ideological belief in social Darwinism provided the initial justification and institutional structure for the persecution.<br><br>Furthermore, Interpretation 2 is highly accurate in its analysis of the role of the police state and the limits of public conformity. As the historian notes, active resistance to sterilisation was minimal because dissent was swiftly crushed by the Gestapo. However, when the regime secretly transitioned to the compulsory sterilisation policy in late 1939—using carbon monoxide gas to systematically murder disabled children and adults—the conformity of the public began to fracture. From my own knowledge, when families realized that their relatives were dying under suspicious circumstances, protests arose, most notably led by Catholic Bishop Clemens August von Galen in 1941. This forced Hitler to temporarily halt the official compulsory sterilisation program, proving that public acceptance relied entirely on keeping the lethal reality of these policies hidden.<br><br>In conclusion, while biological ideology (as emphasized in Interpretation 1) provided the pseudo-scientific framework for sterilisation, it was the economic utility argument highlighted in Interpretation 2 that the regime used to build public acceptance. By framing the disabled as a financial burden during a period of national sacrifice, the Nazis successfully turned ordinary citizens against the most vulnerable, before resorting to absolute secrecy when transitioning to mass murder.",
